What I Built: Agentic Scaffolding Engine

I created a custom, reusable CLI command named `create-ai-agent` on my local machine. It instantly bootstraps an isolated AI Engineering environment equipped with a headless browser and Model Context Protocol (MCP) servers.

How to trigger a new environment:

```

# Open any terminal and type:

create-ai-agent [project-name]

# To start the agent in that new folder:

cd [project-name]

./start.sh

```

Architecture Generated:

1. `orchestrator.py`: The AI Brain (Antigravity engine).

2. `mcp_server.py`: The Robotic Hands (Playwright Browser Controller).

3. `start.sh`: The single power switch to boot the system.

When to Use This:

• Yes: Building an autonomous assistant (AEOS), scraping complex dynamic websites, or needing an AI to interact with software using MCP.



• No: Static calculations, building predictable ledgers, or if stable REST APIs already exist.



What We Built: AI Engineering Operating System (AEOS)

Here is a simple, step-by-step explanation of our AI assistant platform:

1. The "Brain" (The Core System) We created an "AI Engineering Operating System." Instead of just a chatbot, this system is designed to actually do tasks on your computer.

2. Understanding and Planning (Sensory & Intelligence) The AI takes a simple request in plain English, thinks about it, and breaks it down into a structured, step-by-step to-do list.

3. Memory We set up two types of memory so it learns over time:

• Short-term memory: To keep track of current tasks.

• Long-term memory: To remember past interactions, rules, and experiences.

4. "Hands" to Use the Computer (Execution Layer) The AI can take actions on its own: open files, run commands, and open a web browser to navigate, click, and type—just like a human.

5. A Safety Net (Governance Layer) We built a "Human-in-the-Loop" safety system. If the AI is about to do something risky, it pauses and asks for permission. It will not proceed until you manually click "Approve."

6. Web Automation Testing We ran live tests proving the system works. The AI successfully navigated to Google Colab to create a project, and demonstrated opening a local coding environment to type and run code autonomously.

Summary: We built a smart, autonomous AI assistant that can browse the web and control a computer safely, always asking for your permission before doing anything critical.



aicarrierplatform
What This Project Does (in Plain English)
Imagine you are looking for your next career move. This project builds a Smart Career Intelligence Hub—a secure personal career advisor powered by Artificial Intelligence (AI).
Here is what it does for you:
Creates Your Secure Profile: It securely stores your work history, what you achieved, and the skills you have. It has a locked door—only you can register, log in, and view or edit your own details.
AI Verification of Your Skills: Instead of just letting you list skills without proof, the system acts like a verification checker. It reviews your work achievements and uses AI (powered by Google Gemini) to confirm if the work you did actually proves you have that skill.
Benchmarks You Against a Job (Gap Analysis): If you find a job you want to apply for, you paste the job description into the system. The AI compares your verified profile against it and gives you a Match Score (e.g., 70% Match), lists which skills you are missing, and suggests recommendations.
Customized Career Coach: Once it knows your skill gaps, the AI acts as a career coach. It builds a friendly step-by-step learning path showing you what to learn, where to look for tutorials, and how long it should take to close the gap and get ready for the job.


A Look at One of the Files: business-rules.md
This file is the "Rulebook" for the platform. It sets the foundational business guidelines in plain language that the system must follow.
Here are the rules defined inside it:
markdown
# Business Rules
1. **Skill Verification:** A skill is only "Validated" if ai_validated_confidence >= 0.7.
2. **Temporal Integrity:** No end_date before start_date in Experience.
3. **Immutability:** Identity updates create new snapshots.
4. **Evidence Linkage:** Skills must have a foreign key to a project/experience.
What these rules mean in practice:
Rule 1: When the AI evaluates your achievements, it scores its confidence from 0.0 (no confidence) to 1.0 (certainty). A skill is only marked as green/validated if the score is at least 0.7.
Rule 2: You cannot accidentally enter that you finished a job before you started it.
Rule 3: Every time you update your profile, the system logs it as a new snapshot with the current time.
Rule 4: You can't just claim a skill out of thin air; it must be linked directly to a job experience or project you entered.





Recovery (If the command stops working): The script lives at `~/.local/bin/create-ai-agent` (with a `.cmd` wrapper on Windows). If it fails, check that `C:\Users\Lenovo\.local\bin` is still in the Windows User PATH environment variable.
